# A generic circuit breaker: watch any rule, at a threshold of its own,
# without editing the rule being watched. Three pieces, none of them new
# engine machinery:
#
#   `dormant`/`due`   already the disable switch (`core/attention.py:_dormant`)
#   triggers          already see every SELECTION of a rule, even a
#                     no-op reselection -- `_intercept` runs before the
#                     "already believed, skip" check in `_apply`
#   lanes             already a guaranteed turn per round, independent of
#                     who wins `main`'s selection that tick
#
# Why all three are load-bearing, found by testing rather than reasoning:
#
#   A RULE REFERENCE IS TAINTED. `<watched-rule>`'s own graph node has
#   `has_var = True` -- a rule's reification structurally contains its own
#   antecedent/consequent variables, and `has_var` propagates. So a
#   variable bound to a rule (`$r`) cannot be written into a NEW fact
#   safely: a trigger's own addition containing it is silently dropped
#   (`_obey`'s "still generic, not an instruction" check), and
#   `no dormant($r)` silently fails to MATCH at all, guard included. Route
#   "which rule" through a plain, untainted TAG instead
#   (`watched(<R>, some_tag)`), and count/guard against the tag. `$r` is
#   only ever written where the engine's own `_dormant` requires the real
#   node: the final `+dormant($r)`.
#
#   A RULE THAT ALWAYS WINS STARVES ITS OWN WATCHDOG. Built as an ordinary
#   competing rule, `<trip>` never fired against a rule that always
#   matches: the runaway rule keeps winning `main`'s one-selection-per-tick
#   arbitration forever, and an equally-ranked competitor never gets a
#   turn -- including the one rule that would turn it off. A LANE fixes
#   this: `<trip>` in its own lane gets a turn every ROUND regardless of
#   what `main` selects that tick (`docs/tools-approval.md`'s sibling
#   finding: consuming your own trigger is not enough when something ELSE
#   is what needs to act).
#
# To watch a rule: `fact +watched(<the-rule>, a-tag)`, `fact +tries(a-tag, 0)`,
# `fact +threshold(a-tag, N)`. Everything below is shared by every watched
# rule; adding one costs three facts, not a new rule.

fact +intercepts(<watchdog>, after)
rule <watchdog> = implies(
    { +producing($r, $c), +watched($r, $tag), +tries($tag, $n), plus($n, 1) as $n2 },
    { -tries($tag, $n), +tries($tag, $n2) } )

rule <trip> = implies(
    { +watched($r, $tag), +tries($tag, $n), +threshold($tag, $max),
      at_least($n, $max) as $over, no dormant_tag($tag) },
    { +dormant($r), +dormant_tag($tag) } )
fact +lane(<trip>, watchdog)
fact +lane_order(watchdog, 1)
